@title Soil sensor provisioning
@subtitle How a sensor gets provisioned at Brightleaf Greenhouses sites
@author IoT platform team
@date 2026-10-14

Brightleaf is rolling out soil sensors to 31 sites. This page shows field engineers how a sensor gets from pairing to its first telemetry.

!! **Never photograph the one-time token QR code.** Anyone holding the token can claim the device.

## Provisioning sequence

diagram prov sequence title="Sensor provisioning"
prov: sensor "Sensor" svc
prov: gateway "Gateway" svc
prov: ps "Provisioning Service" svc
prov: ca "Certificate Authority" ext
prov: reg "Device Registry" db
prov: sensor -> gateway : BLE pairing request with serial
prov: gateway -> ps : claim device with one-time token
prov: ps -> reg : look up serial and token
prov: reg --> ps : record, status unclaimed
prov: ps -> ca : certificate signing request
prov: ca --> ps : signed certificate, valid 365 days
prov: ps -> reg : mark device active
prov: ps --> gateway : certificate and MQTT endpoint
prov: gateway -> sensor : install certificate
prov: sensor -> gateway : first telemetry over TLS

## Limits and timeouts

table = item,value; One-time token validity,24 hours; Pairing window on the sensor,90 seconds; Signing key type,ECDSA P-256; Claim attempts before the token is locked,3; Certificate lifetime,365 days

## When provisioning fails

table = problem,what to do; Token expired,Request a new token from the dashboard; Pairing window closed,Press and hold the sensor button for 5 seconds to reopen it; Token locked after 3 attempts,Contact IoT support at #iot-support

!! **Reminder: never photograph the one-time token QR code.**
